<h1>Likutay Halachos — Hilchos Tefillin, Halacha 5<br><span style="font-size:18px; color:#7a622e;">Part C: Sections 18–25</span></h1>

<!-- =============== Section 18 =============== -->
<h3>§ 18</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of Hashem's praise for <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Shor'rareich agan hasahar al yechsar hamezeg</span>" — "Your navel is a round basin; let the blended wine not be lacking" <span class="source">(Shir HaShirim 7:3)</span>. Our Sages expounded <span class="source">(Sanhedrin 37a)</span> on the seventy members of the <span class="key-term">Sanhedrin</span>, who would sit in a semicircle like the moon. "<span class="transliteration">Shor'rareich</span>" — this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">retzuah</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> that descends from the knot of the head down to the navel, which is drawn from the aspect of the kingship of <span class="key-term">Dovid Mashiach</span> who lives and endures — the aspect of the knot of the head. From there the strap extends down to the navel: the aspect of "<span class="transliteration">Shor'rareich agan hasahar</span>" — the seventy <span class="key-term">Sanhedrin</span>, corresponding to the <span class="bold-concept">seventy faces of Torah</span>. Dovid sits at their head, as our Sages expounded on the verse "<span class="transliteration">Yosheiv basheves tachk'moni rosh</span>" <span class="source">(Shmuel II 23:8)</span>.</p>

<p>All the seventy <span class="key-term">Sanhedrin</span> — the seventy faces of Torah — receive from <span class="key-term">Dovid Mashiach</span>, whose entire <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> comes from the elders — the <span class="key-term">Tikkunei D'ikna Kadisha</span>, the splendor of the countenance, the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">stories of primordial years</span>, from which all seventy faces of Torah are drawn, as explained in the Torah "<span class="transliteration">Pasach Rabbi Shimon</span>" <span class="source">(LM I:60)</span>. Through them, one awakens from <span class="key-term">sheinah</span> <span class="bracket-def">[sleep]</span> — the departure of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. Through the elders and the stories of primordial years, all seventy faces of Torah are drawn and one awakens from sleep.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">seventy years that Dovid HaMelech lived</span>. Therefore he minimized sleep exceedingly, as our Sages said <span class="source">(Sukkah 26b)</span>: Dovid never slept more than sixty breaths — so that he should not taste the taste of death. For sleep is one-sixtieth of death, drawn through the sin of <span class="key-term">Adam HaRishon</span>, who ate from the <span class="key-term">Eitz HaDa'as</span> <span class="bracket-def">[Tree of Knowledge]</span> and blemished the <span class="key-term">Eitz HaChayim</span> <span class="bracket_def">[Tree of Life]</span> — which is the aspect of the light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. For <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = life = the <span class="key-term">Eitz HaChayim</span>, of which it says: "<span class="transliteration">V'achal v'chai l'olam</span>" — "And he shall eat and live forever" <span class="source">(Bereishis 3:22)</span>.</p>

<p>Had Adam not sinned, he would have merited true life — <span class="bold-concept">eternal life, long life while still in the body</span>: while still in the body he could have been included in the <span class="key-term">Ein Sof</span> forever in <span class="key-term">ratzo vashov</span> <span class="bracket-def">[running and returning]</span>, living long life — renewing his <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> forever in the aspect of the renewal of life of the uppermost elder, the Blind Beggar, who is always old and always <span class="key-term">yanak</span>. But after sinning, eating from the <span class="key-term">Eitz HaDa'as Tov vaRa</span>, being driven from <span class="key-term">Gan Eden</span>, and having the contamination of the serpent (<span class="zohar-quote">mishka d'chivya</span>) cling to his body — now it is impossible to live in the body forever. One can only merit eternal life <span class="bold-concept">through death</span>, which is a great good: "<span class="transliteration">V'hinei tov me'od — zeh hamaves</span>" — "Behold, it is very good — this is death" <span class="source">(Bereishis Rabbah 9)</span>. Through death (= sleep), the brain is renewed, the body is purified, and at the resurrection one will rise in a pure body completely cleansed of the serpent's contamination. Then one will merit to receive new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, the <span class="key-term">Eitz HaChayim</span> — long life: life that is added each moment, where the more one ages the more one begins to live anew. This is the life of the Blind Beggar. This is the essential <span class="bold-concept">eternal life</span> of the World to Come — for constant, unchanging pleasure is not pleasure and not truly called life. Only when one merits to live <span class="key-term">chiyus chadash</span> each moment — this is the essential long, eternal life the Tzaddikim will merit after the resurrection. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 19 =============== -->
<h3>§ 19</h3>

<p>Even now in this world, the Tzaddikim who truly serve Hashem draw upon themselves eternal life through Torah, <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>, and <span class="key-term">ma'asim tovim</span>. Though their entire aspiration is to live always the long life described above — renewing their <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> with added holiness, new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each moment — nevertheless, it is impossible in this world to live this long life without interruption. The brain must become weary. Therefore one must <span class="bold-concept">sleep</span>, and <span class="bold-concept">sleep is a great good</span>: by completely withdrawing the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> when necessary and sleeping a little, one gives rest to the brain. Through this, one is renewed in the morning and merits to receive new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — to begin living anew.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> that one merits in the morning specifically after sleep, as brought in the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span>: through the sleep of the night and through awakening at <span class="key-term">chatzos</span> <span class="bracket-def">[midnight]</span> to engage in Torah, the lights of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> emerge in the morning. These are the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">reshimu</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[imprint]</span> of yesterday's <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> that were renewed during the night's sleep. Through this, one afterward receives during <span class="key-term">tefillah</span> complete, entirely new <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — the essential life, the aspect of long life.</p>

<p>Examine the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span> and understand well — for all the <span class="key-term">kavanos</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are included in our words in a wondrous way, such that each person can receive from them new awakening and wondrous yearning and great, true, and proper counsel at all times for serving the Creator — each person according to his level, his place, and his moment. <span class="bold-concept">Even the lowest of the low can remind himself of Hashem at all times, in any place, however he may be.</span></p>

<p>For now, it is impossible to merit the long life of renewing one's brain and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each moment except through sleep. But the Tzaddikim minimize sleep exceedingly — each one only as much as is necessary for the renewal of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. Therefore Dovid never slept more than sixty breaths — for he lived the long life described above: new life each moment. He could not sleep more than the barest minimum required for long life.</p>

<p>Therefore the kingship of Dovid is compared to the <span class="bold-concept">moon</span>, which renews itself each time — hinting at <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus Dovid</span>, which is comprised of all Yisrael who must renew their <span class="key-term">avodah</span> each time, so that they will merit to be renewed in the future and live the long life described above. As we say in <span class="key-term">Kiddush Levanah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">V'laLevanah amar shetischadesh ateres tiferes la'amusei vaten</span>" — "And to the moon He said that it should renew itself, a crown of glory for those borne from the womb" — who are destined to renew themselves like it, to glorify their Creator on account of the honor of His kingship. Then we say: "<span class="transliteration">Dovid Melech Yisrael chai v'kayam</span>" — for this is the essential <span class="key-term">chiyus</span>: renewal each time.</p>

<p>Therefore <span class="bold-concept">Yisrael counts by the moon</span>, from which the months derive <span class="source">(Chullin 60b)</span> — a hint that we must renew ourselves each time, living new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each day and each moment in serving the Creator. For we never grow old — even if we live a thousand years: "<span class="transliteration">Ki elef shanim b'einecha k'yom esmol</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 90:4)</span>. All our days are counted by months. Every day of the month is counted toward the month — the 2nd of the month, the 3rd, and so on — and then a new month begins. All our days are counted by "<span class="key-term">chodesh</span>" <span class="bracket-def">[month]</span> — the very word for "new." "<span class="transliteration">Mechadesh chodashim</span>" — "He who renews the months" — <span class="bold-concept">"<span class="transliteration">mechadesh</span>" precisely</span>. All the days of Yisrael — the holy nation who received the Torah, which is eternal life ("<span class="transliteration">Ki hu chayecha</span>") — all their days are in the aspect of "<span class="key-term">chodesh</span>": new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each moment. This is the essential long, eternal life.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 20 =============== -->
<h3>§ 20</h3>

<p>All this is merited through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. Therefore the kingship of <span class="key-term">Dovid</span> is bound there — in the <span class="key-term">kesher shel Tefillin</span>. From there descends a holy <span class="key-term">retzuah</span> to the navel — the beginning of man's <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> and birth when he enters the air of the world. The beginning of his separation from his mother's womb to begin living on his own is at the <span class="bold-concept">cutting of the umbilical cord</span> — then he is separated and begins new life, receiving <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> independently for the first time.</p>

<p>The essential illumination of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> drawn upon us through the <span class="key-term">retzuos</span> comes through the brain of the elder who remembers all this — from whom we receive the power to renew our <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each time as if born today and having our umbilical cord just cut. From there is drawn the <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of man, comprised of <span class="bold-concept">seventy years</span> — the days of man's life: "<span class="transliteration">Yemai sh'nosseinu bahem shiv'im shanah</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 90:10)</span> — the years of <span class="key-term">Dovid HaMelech</span> who lived seventy years, corresponding to the seventy faces of Torah, as explained in "<span class="transliteration">Pasach Rabbi Shimon</span>" <span class="source">(LM I:60)</span>. All the seventy faces — the <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of the seventy years — are drawn from the elders (stories of primordial years), whose illumination reaches us through the youngest elder who draws the renewal of <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> by virtue of his extraordinarily great brain that remembers even the cutting of the umbilical cord. The <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of the deeper elders cannot be revealed outwardly.</p>

<p>This is "<span class="transliteration">Shor'rareich agan hasahar</span>" — the moon that renews itself each time, which is itself the seventy <span class="key-term">Sanhedrin</span>. They are the seventy faces, the seventy years, received through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, through the elders, through the <span class="key-term">retzuah</span> descending to the navel — through which one merits to renew his <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each time. This is the essential <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of the entire seventy years. For if one does not renew his <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each time, he has not truly lived seventy years. The essential <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> is <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> — "<span class="transliteration">Ki hu chayecha.</span>" When one does not add holiness and <span class="key-term">da'as</span> each day, his days are short — who knows if all his seventy years will amount to even a single day? Therefore the complete <span class="key-term">reshaim</span> who do not keep the Torah at all are called "<span class="bold-concept">dead in their lifetime</span>" — for they are truly dead, since they do not keep the Torah which is the essential life. Even one who serves Hashem a little — only the days of his service and good deeds are counted among his days of life. All his <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> may not amount to more than one day.</p>

<p>But the essential life is the life of <span class="key-term">Dovid HaMelech</span>, who merited to live seventy complete years in truth — new life each moment. This is why in <span class="key-term">Kiddush Levanah</span>, regarding the renewal of <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> (the essence of the moon's renewal = <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span> = <span class="key-term">Malchus Dovid</span>), we say: "<span class="transliteration">...shetischadesh ateres tiferes</span>" — "<span class="key-term">Ateres Tiferes</span>" precisely = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, which are called <span class="key-term">Ateres Tiferes</span> <span class="bracket-def">[Crown of Glory]</span>, as brought regarding the blessing "<span class="transliteration">Oteir Yisrael b'Sifarah</span>" — "Who crowns Yisrael with glory" — which hints at <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. Through <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is the essential renewal of <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> = the renewal of the moon.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of the holiness of the <span class="key-term">bechor</span> — the opening of the first <span class="key-term">parshah</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Kadeish li chol bechor.</span>" One must redeem the <span class="key-term">bechor</span> from the <span class="key-term">Kohen</span> to draw the renewal of <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> and <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. The essential <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Pidyon HaBen</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[Redemption of the Firstborn]</span> is from <span class="key-term">ben chodesh</span> <span class="bracket-def">[one month old]</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Ufeduyav miben chodesh tifdeh</span>" <span class="source">(Bamidbar 18:16)</span> — "<span class="transliteration">miben chodesh</span>" precisely — for the essential redemption is for the sake of renewal of <span class="key-term">chiyus</span>, which is the aspect of "<span class="key-term">chodesh</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">mechadesh chodashim.</span>"</p>

<!-- =============== Section 21 =============== -->
<h3>§ 21</h3>

<p>For Dovid had <span class="bold-concept">no <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of his own at all</span>. He was destined to be a <span class="key-term">nefel</span> <span class="bracket-def">[stillbirth]</span>. Only <span class="key-term">Adam HaRishon</span> gave him seventy years from his own lifespan, as our Sages said. The <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of <span class="key-term">Adam HaRishon</span> was drawn from <span class="key-term">Arich Anpin</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Arichas Apayim</span>, the stories of primordial years. As brought in the Writings, the <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of the early generations came from <span class="key-term">Arich Anpin</span> — therefore they lived very long. All the stories the Torah tells about the early generations are aspects of the stories of primordial years — the <span class="bold-concept">splendor of the countenance</span> — from which all seventy faces of Torah receive. Therefore the Torah <span class="bold-concept">begins with these holy stories before explicating the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span></span> — for all the <span class="key-term">mitzvos</span> of Torah, comprised in the seventy faces, need to receive from the splendor of the countenance — the beauty of Adam — the stories of primordial years.</p>

<p>The essential <span class="key-term">chiyus d'Kedushah</span> drawn from there is to renew one's <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each moment, to begin living anew. This is "<span class="transliteration">Ani hayom yelidticha</span>" — said about <span class="key-term">Dovid Mashiach</span>: Dovid merited that the matter was reversed from one extreme to the other. Initially he had no <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> at all and was destined to be an actual <span class="key-term">nefel</span>. The matter was reversed and he merited to live seventy years from <span class="key-term">Adam HaRishon</span>'s years — and he lived all seventy years in the aspect of <span class="bold-concept">new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each moment, as if he had never yet lived at all</span>. He was at all times like a <span class="key-term">nefel</span> just now born, having no <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of his own, needing to receive new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> through Hashem's kindness. And so every moment and instant — and so he lived all seventy years. This is the essential life.</p>

<p>Until through his <span class="key-term">avodah</span> and toil in this aspect — striving all his days not to sleep away his days but to add holiness and <span class="key-term">da'as</span> each moment, renewing his <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each hour — he merited long and eternal life forever: <span class="key-term">Dovid Melech Yisrael chai v'kayam</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. For <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is from the word "<span class="transliteration">v'niflinu</span>"</span> — "<span class="transliteration">V'niflinu ani v'amecha</span>" — "I and Your people shall be distinguished" <span class="source">(Shemos 33:16)</span> — said when Hashem revealed the Thirteen Attributes = the light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = "<span class="transliteration">V'raisa es achorai</span>" = the knot of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. There is the root of <span class="key-term">Dovid Mashiach</span>'s <span class="key-term">neshamah</span> — who was destined to be a <span class="key-term">nefel</span> but now merited to live new life each moment as if born today. This is <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = <span class="key-term">v'niflinu</span>: the matter was reversed — from <span class="key-term">nefel</span> to <span class="key-term">v'niflinu</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = long life.</p>

<p>Therefore <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> is called <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Bar Nafli</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[son of the fallen]</span> — for <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> will merit the life described above: "<span class="transliteration">Ani hayom yelidticha.</span>"</p>

<!-- =============== Section 22 =============== -->
<h3>§ 22</h3>

<p>This is: "<span class="transliteration">Someich Hashem l'chol hanoflim</span>" — "Hashem supports all who fall" <span class="source">(Tehillim 145:14)</span>. These are those who fall from <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span>. Hashem supports and awakens them from their sleep through the <span class="bold-concept">Tzaddikei HaDor</span>, who are the aspect of <span class="key-term">Dovid Mashiach</span> — who merited to transform from <span class="key-term">nefel</span> to long life, beginning to live anew each time. This is <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = <span class="key-term">v'niflinu</span>. Through this they enliven and sustain all who have fallen, that they should not fall in their minds and not despair of their souls' redemption — until they merit to return to Hashem. Then <span class="bold-concept">the fall and descent is itself the ultimate ascent</span>. The essential fall is precisely for the sake of beginning to live anew, as explained elsewhere.</p>

<p>As our Rebbe said <span class="source">(LM I:261)</span>: that a person falls from his <span class="key-term">avodah</span> is from Heaven — for <span class="bold-concept">the distancing is the beginning of the drawing close</span>. Therefore he fell so that he would awaken more to draw close to Hashem. His counsel: <span class="bold-concept">begin anew to enter <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> as if he had never begun at all. This is a great principle in <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> — that one must literally begin anew each day.</span> [End of quote.]</p>

<p>Thus precisely through the fall one begins to live anew = <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = the long life of Dovid who was transformed from <span class="key-term">nefel</span> to long life. This is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Geulah</span></span> that will come through <span class="key-term">Dovid Mashiach</span> speedily in our days — when <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span> will rise from her fall, as our Sages expounded <span class="source">(Berachos 4b)</span> on this verse (for this reason there is no <span class="key-term">Nun</span> in <span class="key-term">Ashrei</span> — yet Dovid supported it through <span class="key-term">Ruach HaKodesh</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Someich Hashem l'chol hanoflim</span>"). In the West they resolve it thus: <span class="bold-concept">"She fell and shall not fall again — arise!"</span></p>

<p>For all the fall of <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span> is that Yisrael fall from their <span class="key-term">avodah</span> — and the <span class="key-term">Geulah</span> depends on <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> <span class="source">(Yoma 86b)</span>. The essential support is through Dovid, who lived the long life — through which he enlivens, strengthens, and supports all who fall, so that they don't despair: "<span class="transliteration">Al tismachi oyvasi li, ki nafalti kamti, ki eishev bachoshech Hashem or li</span>" — "Rejoice not over me, my enemy — though I fell, I rose; though I sit in darkness, Hashem is my light" <span class="source">(Michah 7:8)</span>.</p>

<p>This is why the <span class="key-term">Kohen Gadol</span> prayed on <span class="key-term">Yom HaKippurim</span> in the <span class="key-term">Beis HaMikdash</span>: "<span class="transliteration">V'lo tapil ishah pri vitnah, v'lo ye'adi avid shultan midveis Yehudah</span>" — "May no woman miscarry, and may the scepter not depart from the house of Yehudah." Both are one: that there should be no actual <span class="key-term">nefel</span>, nor any fall — and both are merited through the enduring kingship of Dovid, who was transformed from <span class="key-term">nefel</span> to long life. <span class="key-term">Yom HaKippurim</span> is the essential <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> — rising from the fall (<span class="key-term">nefel</span>) to renew one's days: "<span class="transliteration">Hashiveinu... chadeish yameinu</span>." After <span class="key-term">Yom Kippur</span> comes <span class="key-term">Sukkos</span> = "<span class="transliteration">Akim es Sukkas Dovid hanofales</span>" — "I will raise the fallen booth of Dovid" — nullifying the aspect of <span class="key-term">nefilah</span>, raising and enlivening through "<span class="transliteration">Someich Hashem l'chol hanoflim.</span>" After <span class="key-term">Sukkos</span> comes <span class="key-term">Shemini Atzeres</span> = <span class="key-term">Malchus Dovid</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Zeh ya'atzor b'ami</span>" — "He will restrain My people" <span class="source">(Shmuel I 9:17)</span>. As our Rebbe said <span class="source">(LM I:48)</span>: then the <span class="key-term">Nukva</span> restrains the drop so that it should not miscarry — as written in the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 23 =============== -->
<h3>§ 23</h3>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span></span>. <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — the renewal one merits each day through sleep, when the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> are renewed within <span class="key-term">emunah</span> — this is <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as brought in the Torah "<span class="transliteration">Ashrei ha'am</span>" <span class="source">(LM I:35)</span>. <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the aspect of the holiness of <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Tamid einei Hashem Elokecha bah, mereishis hashanah v'ad acharis shanah</span>" <span class="source">(Devarim 11:12)</span>. This is <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = holiness of <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span> = "<span class="transliteration">V'hayah ki yevi'acha</span>" in the <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>.</p>

<p>The essential holiness of <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> is that we merit complete <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> — it is the first of the <span class="key-term">Aseres Yemei Teshuvah</span>: to renew the days that passed in darkness and to add holiness and <span class="key-term">avodah</span> anew. This is: "<span class="transliteration">Chadeish aleinu shanah tovah</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">Chadeish</span>" precisely — that we merit a <span class="bold-concept">genuinely new year</span>. Each year is a unique face among the seventy faces of Torah. Therefore each year one must merit entirely new faces — to live new life from now on = long life.</p>

<p>This is why we multiply our prayers on <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> for <span class="bold-concept">life</span> — many, many times in each <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Zachreinu l'chayim... ukesov l'chayim... zocheir yetzurav l'chayim b'rachamim</span>..." For the essential "<span class="key-term">chayim</span>" is the long life of the Blind Beggar who merited the ultimate memory in perfection — from whom the holiness of <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> is drawn = the holiness of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> = "<span class="transliteration">Ul'zikaron bein einecha.</span>" Therefore <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> is called <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Yom HaZikaron</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[Day of Remembrance]</span> — for its essential holiness is from the <span class="key-term">zikaron</span> of the elder. All our requests for life on <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> — our intention is true, eternal life: renewing <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each moment. "<span class="transliteration">Chadeish</span>" precisely.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 24 =============== -->
<h3>§ 24</h3>

<p>This is the great <span class="key-term">Chesed</span> Hashem did for us — that He established <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> on <span class="key-term">Rosh Chodesh</span></span>. This is a great <span class="key-term">Chesed</span>, as explained in the Torah "<span class="transliteration">Tik'u</span> I" <span class="source">(LM II)</span>. (We also heard more about this from the awesome <span class="key-term">ma'aseh</span> related to this regarding the <span class="key-term">kisei</span> <span class="bracket-def">[throne]</span>, which has not yet been published.) For we need Hashem to renew the year upon us in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Rosh Chodesh</span> — the renewal of <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each time = <span class="key-term">Malchus Dovid Mashiach</span>.</p>

<p>We greatly request on <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> that His kingship — holy kingship — be revealed. As we say many times: "<span class="transliteration">V'simloch... umoloch...</span>" and "<span class="transliteration">Va'arichas ner l'ven Yishai meshichecha u'tzmichas keren l'Dovid avdecha bimheirah v'yameinu.</span>" The kingship of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> is the long life we request on <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> = <span class="key-term">Kiddush HaChodesh</span>. Therefore He established <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> on <span class="key-term">Rosh Chodesh</span>.</p>

<p>This is what our Sages said regarding <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> being on <span class="key-term">Rosh Chodesh</span>: "<span class="bold-concept">One <span class="key-term">zikaron</span> rises for both</span>" — "<span class="key-term">zikaron</span>" precisely. The holiness of <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> (= <span class="key-term">Rosh Chodesh</span>) — to renew the year with new <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> = long life — all this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">zikaron</span>.</p>

<p>This is also explained in the words of our Rebbe <span class="source">(LM II:40)</span>, beginning "<span class="transliteration">Mi she'yode'a mei'Eretz Yisrael</span>" — where it is explained that <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, <span class="key-term">Eretz Yisrael</span>, and <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span> are one aspect</span>.</p>

<!-- =============== Section 25 =============== -->
<h3>§ 25</h3>

<p>Behold, the sum total of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> is the aspect of <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Mochin Chayim</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[living brains]</span>: to begin living anew each time — whether Tzaddik, <span class="key-term">beinoni</span>, or <span class="key-term">rasha</span>. As long as the name <span class="key-term">Yisrael</span> is called upon him, he must begin anew each day. This is: "<span class="transliteration">V'atem had'veikim BaHashem Elokeichem — chayim kulchem hayom</span>" — "You who cling to Hashem your G-d — you are all alive today" <span class="source">(Devarim 4:4)</span> — said regarding the holy <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. "<span class="transliteration">Chayim kulchem hayom</span>" — "<span class="transliteration">hayom</span>" precisely — one must renew <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> each and every day as if beginning to live today.</p>

<p>This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> and <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span>. It is brought in the <span class="key-term">Kavanos</span> that <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span></span> are the aspect of three Names: <span class="key-term">Ehyeh Havayah Ehyeh</span>, which have the numerical value of <span class="key-term">Chayim</span> <span class="bracket-def">[= 68]</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>. And <span class="bold-concept"><span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span></span> are the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> — therefore they are three Names: <span class="key-term">Ehyeh Havayah Adnus</span>, which equal <span class="key-term">Yabok</span> <span class="bracket-def">[= 112]</span>.</p>

<p>It is explained there that the <span class="bold-concept">light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> emerges through the sleep of the night</span>, when the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> are renewed within <span class="key-term">emunah</span> (= <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>). By arising at <span class="key-term">chatzos</span> and mourning the destruction of the <span class="key-term">Beis HaMikdash</span> and engaging in Torah — through this, the point of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>-<span class="key-term">emunah</span>, which descended during the night to all the lower worlds to extract holy sparks (the <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> who fell through their sins, to instill in them <span class="key-term">hirhurei teshuvah</span> and raise them and return them to Hashem) — is raised up. The Tzaddikim and G-d-fearing, through <span class="key-term">Tikun Chatzos</span> and Torah study at night, raise the point of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> until it ascends at dawn and sits on the <span class="bold-concept">left arm</span> — its place. Then <span class="key-term">Z'eir Anpin</span> shines into it the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of its <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> that remained in his heart — for the essential <span class="key-term">Mochin d'Gadlus</span> exist only during <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>; afterward they depart, leaving only the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span>. This is the light of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> remaining all day. At night, even the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> departs — therefore <span class="bold-concept">there are no <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> at night</span>. The <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>'s <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> also departs at night, except that this <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> does not depart completely — it remains in the heart: "<span class="transliteration">Simeini k'chosam al libecha, k'chosam al zero'echa</span>" — "Set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm" <span class="source">(Shir HaShirim 8:6)</span>.</p>

<p>Then in the morning, he shines the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> into her on the left arm — this is <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span>. When <span class="key-term">Z'eir Anpin</span> sees that she already has her <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> — the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — he is <span class="bold-concept">aroused with <span class="key-term">kinah</span></span> <span class="bracket-def">[jealousy]</span>: "<span class="transliteration">Kashah kaSh'ol kinah</span>" — "Jealousy is fierce as the grave." Then he takes back his own <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> renewed during the night — and this is <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span>.</p>

<p>Thus: <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> = the shining of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> (<span class="key-term">Z'eir Anpin</span>). <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> = the shining of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> (<span class="key-term">emunah</span>). The essential <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>, <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>, and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> are drawn through the <span class="bold-concept">true Tzaddikei HaDor</span> — the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">chiyus</span> of the entire world. Each Tzaddik HaDor is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Moshe-Mashiach</span>. <span class="key-term">Moshe</span> is the <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> — the <span class="key-term">Tefillin-Mochin</span>. As our Rebbe said <span class="source">(LM I:38)</span>: the true Tzaddik is the aspect of the <span class="bold-concept">river that goes out from Eden</span> to water the Garden — "<span class="transliteration">Umisham yipared v'hayah l'arba'ah rashim</span>" — "And from there it divides into four heads" = the four <span class="key-term">parshiyos</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span>. The <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are drawn through the river from Eden = the true Tzaddik.</p>

<p><span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> = the Tzaddik himself, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Z'eir Anpin</span>. <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> = <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span>, <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>. That is: the Tzaddik's own <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> = <span class="key-term">shel Rosh</span>. The <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Klal Yisrael</span>, whom the Tzaddik illuminates and draws close to Hashem = <span class="key-term">shel Yad</span> — whose essence is <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, the foundation upon which everything depends: "<span class="transliteration">Ba Chavakuk v'he'emidan al achas — v'Tzaddik be'emunaso yichyeh!</span>" — "Chavakuk came and established them on one — and the righteous shall live by his faith!" <span class="source">(Makkos 24a)</span>.</p>

<p>The Tzaddik must withdraw his <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> — even the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> departs during sleep — and nothing remains but the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> remaining in his heart ("<span class="transliteration">k'chosam al libecha</span>"). Then during sleep, <span class="key-term">emunah</span> contracts to a point and descends to all the lower worlds to extract sparks — the <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> who fell through their sins. For them, the Tzaddik must lower himself and withdraw his <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> in order to instill in them complete <span class="key-term">emunah</span> and bring them close to the holy <span class="key-term">emunah</span> and return them to Hashem. All this is the Tzaddik's "sleep" — as understood from our Rebbe's words: that when the Tzaddik engages in mundane conversation and stories, this is the aspect of sleep for him. It is all for the sake of raising and lifting the world to draw them close to Hashem. Since they cannot receive from the Tzaddik's <span class="key-term">Moach</span> directly, he must withdraw his <span class="key-term">Da'as</span>, contract himself to a point, and extract and raise them through the point of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> alone.</p>

<p>Afterward, when he awakens from sleep with all the sparks and <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> he has raised and awakened to Hashem — they must engage in Torah, <span class="key-term">tefillah</span>, and <span class="key-term">Avodas Hashem</span> until they merit to ascend to their place, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> — <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span> sitting on the left arm. Then all the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of all the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">Klal Yisrael</span>, which had departed but remained in the Tzaddik's heart ("<span class="transliteration">k'chosam al libecha</span>") — he shines everything into the point of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> that ascended with all the extractions (the <span class="key-term">neshamos</span> of the <span class="key-term">ba'alei teshuvah</span> whom he raised and returned to Hashem). He shines <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> and <span class="key-term">seichel</span> into them to strengthen them in complete <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, so that they strengthen their <span class="key-term">avodah</span> and do not look at the hindrances of the <span class="key-term">Baal Davar</span> and other obstacles, but pass over everything through the strength of their <span class="key-term">emunah</span> that was greatly strengthened through the <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> he shone into them — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span>. Through this, <span class="key-term">yad keihah</span> — all the hindrances drawn from <span class="key-term">Gevuros</span> and <span class="key-term">dinim</span> — is sweetened. Through the strength of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, they pass over everything — nothing can make them fall. He shines into them the <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> of holy <span class="key-term">emunah</span>: knowing that Hashem is in every place and <span class="bold-concept">there is no obstacle in the world that cannot be broken and there is no despair in the world at all</span>.</p>

<p>When the Tzaddik sees that the point of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> has succeeded and borne fruit — that it has already received <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> (= <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span>), i.e. his efforts have succeeded, having illuminated <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> in the souls he raised and drew close to Hashem until they are strong in their <span class="key-term">emunah</span> to stand their ground and pass over all obstacles — then <span class="bold-concept">the Tzaddik himself is aroused with <span class="key-term">kinah</span> for his students</span>. Seeing that they have received true <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> from him, he is aroused with jealousy — in the aspect of "<span class="transliteration">Umitalmidai yoser mikulam</span>" — "From my students more than from all." Through the students in whom he shone the <span class="key-term">Moach</span> and <span class="key-term">Da'as</span> of holy <span class="key-term">emunah</span> (<span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span> = <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span>), he is aroused with jealousy and remembers: "Where are my own <span class="key-term">Mochin</span>?" Then he takes back the <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> of his <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> that had departed during sleep — and receives this <span class="key-term">reshimu</span> renewed with wondrous novelty. This is <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span>.</p>

<p>As explained in our Rebbe's words elsewhere: first the Tzaddik lowers himself to draw close souls, and afterward he returns to his level with added illumination. From these two aspects — the Tzaddik's own <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> and the <span class="key-term">Mochin</span> of <span class="key-term">emunah</span> that he instills in <span class="key-term">Kneses Yisrael</span> — are drawn <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> and <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Yad</span> for each and every individual, for every aspect includes all aspects.</p>

<p>This is: "<span class="transliteration">Lam'natze'ach l'Dovid l'hazkir</span>" <span class="source">(Tehillim 70:1)</span> — "<span class="transliteration">l'hazkir</span>" — to remind himself, as Rashi explains there: a parable of a king who was angry at his flock... afterward he returned the flock... That is: first the Tzaddik reminds Yisrael to return to Hashem; then, when he sees that his prayer and toil have succeeded and he has returned Yisrael to Hashem — then he <span class="bold-concept">reminds himself</span>. This is <span class="key-term">Tefillin shel Rosh</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">zikaron</span>.</p>


